        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#delusion_nihilistic -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#delusion_nihilistic">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/delusion"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0233677</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>MS</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Nihilistic delusions, also known as délires de négation, are specific psychopathological entities characterized by the delusional belief of being dead, decomposed or annihilated, having lost one&apos;s own internal organs or even not existing entirely as a human being.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#delusion_religious -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#delusion_religious">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/delusion"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0424021</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>MS</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Individuals experiencing religious delusions are preoccupied with religious subjects that are not within the expected beliefs for an individual&apos;s background, including culture, education, and known experiences of religion. These preoccupations are incongruous with the mood of the subject.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#dementia_associated_with_Parkinson -->

