        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A face that is has reduce mobility and an inability to express emotion.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#extremity_limb_rigidity -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#extremity_limb_rigidity">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/limb_rigidity"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0239366</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Increased tone and resistance to passive movement in a limb.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#eye_twitching -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#eye_twitching">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/visual_symptoms"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0850674</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">699377006</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A common cause of eyelid twitching is ocular myokymia. This is benign and does not lead to other problems. Ocular myokymia can be caused by being tired, having too much caffeine, or stress. One cause of persistent, frequent eye twitching is a condition called benign essential blepharospasm.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>Abnormal twitching movements of the eye.</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#facial_asymmetry -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#facial_asymmetry">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/facial_paresis"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C1306710</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#failure_to_thrive -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#failure_to_thrive">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/consitutitional_symptoms"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0015544</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Growth disorder of infants and children due to nutritional and/or emotional deprivation and resulting in loss of weight and delayed physical, emotional, and social development.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#falls_accidental -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#falls_accidental">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/falls"/>
        <Example_1>Has fallen accidently.</Example_1>
        <Example_2>Has been injured by falls.</Example_2>
        <Example_3>Falling by accident.</Example_3>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0000921</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Falls due to slipping or tripping which result in loss or injury.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#falls_frequent -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#falls_frequent">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/falls"/>
        <HPO rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0002359</HPO>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0850703</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#fatiguable_weakness_of_chewing_muscles -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#fatiguable_weakness_of_chewing_muscles">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/weakness_of_jaw_muscles"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C4022590</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A type of weakness of the muscles involved in chewing that occurs after a muscle group is used and lessens if the muscle group has some rest. That is, there is diminution of strength with repetitive muscle actions</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#fatigue_generalized -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#fatigue_generalized">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/fatigue"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0241898</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>It usually refers to nonspecific sense of a low energy level, or the feeling that near exhaustion is reached after relatively little exertion. Fatigue should be distinguished from true neurologic weakness, which describes a reduction in the normal power of one or more muscles</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#favorable_response_to_dopaminergic_medication -->

