        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#seizure_partial -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#seizure_partial">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/seizure"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0751495</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A focal-onset seizure is a type of seizure originating within networks limited to one hemisphere. They may be discretely localized or more widely distributed, and may originate in subcortical structures.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#seizure_simple_partial -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#seizure_simple_partial">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#seizure_partial"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0234974</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A partial (focal) seizure happens when unusual electrical activity affects a small area of the brain. When the seizure does not affect awareness, it is known as a simple partial seizure. Simple partial seizures can be: Motor - affecting the muscles of the body; Sensory - affecting the senses; Autonomic - affecting automatically controlled functions; Psychic - affecting feelings or thoughts. Simple focal seizures are also known as auras.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#sensory_change -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#sensory_change">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/sensory_symptoms"/>
        <synonym_1>changed sensation.</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>altered sensation.</synonym_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C1409715</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>An alteration in sensation.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#sexual_dysfunction -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#sexual_dysfunction">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/urinary_symptoms"/>
        <Example_1>sexual dysfunction</Example_1>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0549622</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>state in which an individual experiences a change in sexual function during the sexual response phases of desire, arousal, and/or orgasm, which is viewed as unsatisfying, unrewarding, or inadequate.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#shock-like_electrical_pain -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#shock-like_electrical_pain">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/pain_electric_shock_sensations"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C4489117</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Nerve pain often feels like a shooting, stabbing or burning sensation. Sometimes it can be as sharp and sudden as an electric shock. People with neuropathic pain are often very sensitive to touch or cold and can experience pain as a result of stimuli that would not normally be painful, such as brushing the skin</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


