        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Sensory</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Sensory loss in the distribution of a cervical root.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/choking -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/choking">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/GI_symptoms"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0008301</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Interference with respiration by compression or obstruction of the larynx or trachea.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/chorea -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/chorea">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/hyperkinesia"/>
        <HPO rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0002072</HPO>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0008489</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">271700006</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Chorea (Greek for dance) refers to widespread arrhythmic involuntary movements of a forcible, jerky and restless fashion. It is a random-appearing sequence of one or more discrete involuntary movements or movement fragments. Movements appear random because of variability in timing, duration or location. Each movement may have a distinct start and end. However, movements may be strung together and thus may appear to flow randomly from one muscle group to another. Chorea can involve the trunk, neck, face, tongue, and extremities.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/choreoathetosis -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/choreoathetosis">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/hyperkinesia"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0085583</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">43105007</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Involuntary movements characterized by both athetosis (inability to sustain muscles in a fixed position) and chorea (widespread jerky arrhythmic movements).</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/chronic -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/chronic">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/course"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0205191</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Course</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">90734009</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Slow, creeping onset, slow progress and long continuance of disease manifestations.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/ciliospinal_reflex_absent -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/ciliospinal_reflex_absent">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/Horners_syndrome"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0558750</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">274603003</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>The ciliospinal reflex (pupillary-skin reflex) consists of dilation of the ipsilateral pupil in response to pain applied to the neck, face, and upper trunk. If the right side of the neck is subjected to a painful stimulus, the right pupil dilates (increases in size 1-2mm from baseline). This reflex is absent in Horner&apos;s syndrome and lesions involving the cervical sympathetic fibers. The enhanced ciliospinal reflex in asymptomatic patients with cluster headache is due to preganglionic sympathetic mechanisms.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/clasp_knife_spasticity -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/clasp_knife_spasticity">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/spasticity"/>
        <Example_1>Clasp knife increase in tone.</Example_1>
        <Example_2>Resistance to extension like a clasp knife.</Example_2>
        <synonym_1>clasp knife phenomenon.</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>clasp knife rigidity.</synonym_2>
        <synonym_3>clasp knife like increase in tone.</synonym_3>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0277820</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">2581006</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>clasp-knife spasticity a type of increased muscle tone due to a pyramidal tract lesion, in which abnormally increased resistance to passive stretch of a muscle abruptly decreases; typically, this appears near the end of the range of joint excursion.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/clonus -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/clonus">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/hyperreflexia"/>
        <Example_1>clonus was present.</Example_1>
        <Example_2>Stretch of the achilles tendon produced clonus.</Example_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0009024</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Reflex</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">36649002</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A series of rhythmic and involuntary muscle contractions (at a frequency of about 5 to 7 Hz) that occur in response to an abruptly applied and sustained stretch.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/clouded_consciousness -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/clouded_consciousness">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_alertness"/>
        <synonym_1>brain fog</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>fogginess</synonym_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0683369</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>MS</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">40917007</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Clouding of consciousness (also known as brain fog or mental fog) is when a person is slightly less wakeful or aware than normal. ... They are not as aware of time or their surroundings and find it difficult to pay attention. People describe this subjective sensation as their mind being &quot;foggy</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/clumsiness -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/clumsiness">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/poor_coordination"/>
        <HPO rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0002312</HPO>
        <synonym_1>imprecise movements</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>awkwardness</synonym_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0233844</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Movements that are imprecise or inaccurate.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>clumsy</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cognitive_impairment -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cognitive_impairment">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cognitive_symptoms"/>
        <Example_1>diminished cognitive abilities</Example_1>
        <Example_2>reduced intellectual capabilities</Example_2>
        <Example_3>reduced intellectual capacity</Example_3>
        <HPO rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0100543</HPO>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0338656</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">386806002</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Diminished or impaired mental and/or intellectual function</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cognitive_symptoms -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cognitive_symptoms">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/symptom"/>
        <Example_1>symptoms of cognition.</Example_1>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C1392787</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Symptoms related to cognitive behavior such as language, memory, reasoning, abstract thought, and visual spatial manipulations.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cogwheel_rigidity -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cogwheel_rigidity">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/rigidity"/>
        <HPO rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0002396</HPO>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0151564</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">55630000</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A type of rigidity in which a muscle responds with cogwheellike jerks to the use of constant force in bending the limb (i.e., it gives way in little, repeated jerks when the muscle is passively stretched).</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/collapse -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/collapse">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/collapsed"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0344329</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">271787007</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>fall down and become unconscious, typically through illness or injury.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/collapsed -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/collapsed">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/consitutitional_symptoms"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0392748</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>fall down and become unconscious, typically through illness or injury.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/coma -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/coma">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/unresponsive"/>
        <synonym_1>comatose</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>coma</synonym_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0009421</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>MS</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">371632003</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A profound state of unconsciousness associated with depressed cerebral activity from which the individual cannot be aroused. Coma generally occurs when there is dysfunction or injury involving both cerebral hemispheres or the brain stem reticular formation.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>comatose</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/complete_deafness -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/complete_deafness">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/deafness"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0018784</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Complete hearing loss.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/complete_hearing_loss -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/complete_hearing_loss">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/deafness"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0581883</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Complete hearing loss.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/complex_partial_seizure -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/complex_partial_seizure">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#seizure_partial"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0149958</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4103001</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A type of focal-onset seizure characterized by impaired awareness. Awareness during a seizure is defined as the patient being fully aware of themself and their environment throughout the seizure, even if immobile. If awareness is impaired at any point during the seizure, the seizure is a focal impaired awareness seizure. The degree of loss of awareness may vary.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/comprehension_dysprosody -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/comprehension_dysprosody">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/dysprosody"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0233744</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>MS</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">112089001</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>An inability to comprehend emotion as conveyed by the prosody of speech.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/concentric_visual_field_loss -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/concentric_visual_field_loss">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/constricted_visual_field"/>
        <synonym_1>concentric narrowing of visual field</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>constricted visual fields</synonym_2>
        <synonym_3>limited peripheral vision</synonym_3>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C4478437</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>An absolute or relative decrease in retinal sensitivity extending from edge (periphery) of the visual field in a concentric pattern. The visual field is the area that is perceived simultaneously by a fixating eye</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/confabulation -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/confabulation">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/loss_of_executive_functions"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0233800</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>MS</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">17842005</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Giving untruthful answers to questions about situations or events that are not recalled due to loss of memory. Confabulation is not a conscious attempt to deceive.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/confusion -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/confusion">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cognitive_symptoms"/>
        <Example_1>confused</Example_1>
        <Example_2>muiddled</Example_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0009676</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">286933003</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A mental state characterized by bewilderment, emotional disturbance, lack of clear thinking, and perceptual disorientation</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>confusional_state; dazed; muddled; clouded_consciousness</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/conjunctival_hyperemia -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/conjunctival_hyperemia">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/visual_symptoms"/>
        <synonym_1>conjunctival injection</synonym_1>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C1761613</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">193894004</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Conjunctival hyperemia is a conjunctival reaction that appears as dilation and redness of the conjunctival vessels. The pattern of hyperemia often appears with the greatest redness at the fornices and fades moving toward the limbus.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/consitutitional_symptoms -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/consitutitional_symptoms">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/symptom"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0009812</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A group of symptoms that affect an individual&apos;s general well being. Representative examples include fever, chills, fatigue, weakness, and weight loss.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/constipation -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/constipation">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#bowel_problems"/>
        <HPO rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0002019</HPO>
        <synonym_1>constipated.</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>constipation.</synonym_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0009806</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Irregular and infrequent or difficult evacuation of the bowels.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/constricted_visual_field -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/constricted_visual_field">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/visual_field_defect"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0235095</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">267628004</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>An absolute or relative decrease in retinal sensitivity extending from edge (periphery) of the visual field in a concentric pattern. The visual field is the area that is perceived simultaneously by a fixating eye.  Narrowed or reduced visual field.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>reduced_visual_field; narrowed_visual_field</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/convergence_retraction_nystagmus -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/convergence_retraction_nystagmus">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/nystagmus_retraction"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C2939429</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">373590007</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Convergence‐retraction nystagmus is an irregular, jerky nystagmus in which both eyeballs rhythmically converge and retract into the orbit, particularly on attempting an upward gaze.1 In humans it is seen as part of Parinaud&apos;s syndrome, also known as dorsal midbrain syndrome, in which a lesion of dorsally located</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/conversion_disorder -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/conversion_disorder">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#abnormal_behavior"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0009946</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>MS</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">20734000</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A disorder whose predominant feature is a loss or alteration in physical functioning that suggests a physical disorder but that is actually a direct expression of a psychological conflict or need.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>hysteria</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/coordination_finding -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/coordination_finding">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#motor_disorders"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0575091</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">298314008</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Coordination is the ability to perform complex movements precisely and accurately.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/corneal_opacity -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/corneal_opacity">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cataract"/>
        <synonym_1>corneal haze</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>corneal subepithelial haze</synonym_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0010038</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Disorder occurring in the central or peripheral area of the cornea. The usual degree of transparency becomes relatively opaque</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cortical_blindness -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cortical_blindness">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/bilateral_hemianopia"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0155320</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">68574006</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A form of loss of vision caused by damage to the visual cortex rather than a defect in the eye.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cough -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cough">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/respiratory_symptoms"/>
        <HPO rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">0012735</HPO>
        <synonym_1>cough</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>coughing</synonym_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0010200</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">272039006</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A sudden, audible expulsion of air from the lungs through a partially closed glottis, preceded by inhalation</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/course -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/course">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#sign"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0547045</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Top</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">246452003</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A description or characterization of the duration of a disease.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_11_finding -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_11_finding">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_finding"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0152180</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">84759007</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>The major part of the spinal accessory nerve (11th cranial nerve) innervates the trapezius muscle and the sternocleidomastoid muscle.  Weakness of these muscles maybe supranuclear, nuclear, or infranuclear in origin.  A special visceral efferent branch to the vagus nerve is not tested clinically.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>Acessory_nerve_disorder</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_12_finding -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_12_finding">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_finding"/>
        <Example_1>twelfth cranial nerve palsy.</Example_1>
        <Example_2>Abnormality of twelfth cranial nerve.</Example_2>
        <synonym_1>hypoglossal nerve palsy</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>abnormality of hypoglossal nerve</synonym_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0234338</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">106162004</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>The twelfth cranial nerve is purely motor and supplies the tongue.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>twelfth_cranial_nerve_finding; hypoglossal_nerve_finding</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_1_finding -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_1_finding">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_finding"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0751937</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">68982002</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Diseases of the first cranial (olfactory) nerve, which usually feature anosmia or other alterations in the sense of smell and taste.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>olfactory_nerve_disorder; first_cranial_nerve_finding; cranial_nerve_I_finding</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_2_3_4_6_vision_disorder -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_2_3_4_6_vision_disorder">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_finding"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0042790</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">128127008</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Cranial nerve 2, 3,4, and 6 are involved in vision.  Eye movements, pupillary reactions, visual fields, and visual acuity may all be affected.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>visual_system_disorder</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_5_finding -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_5_finding">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_finding"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0234274</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">106155008</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Findings related to the fifth cranial nerve; the trigeminal nerve.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>trigeminal_nerve_finding</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_7_finding -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_7_finding">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_finding"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0234289</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">106157000</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Diseases of the facial nerve or nuclei. Pontine disorders may affect the facial nuclei or nerve fascicle. The nerve may be involved intracranially, along its course through the petrous portion of the temporal bone, or along its extracranial course. Clinical manifestations include facial muscle weakness, loss of taste from the anterior tongue, hyperacusis, and decreased lacrimation.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>facial_nerve_finding</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_8_finding -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_8_finding">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_finding"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0001163</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">77949003</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Pathological processes of the vestibulocochlear nerve, including the branches of cochlear and vestibular nerve. Common examples are vestibular neuritis, cochlear neuritis, and acoustic neuroma. Clinical signs are varying degree of hearing loss, vertigo, and tinnitus.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>acoustico-vestibular_nerve_disorders</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_9_10_finding -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_9_10_finding">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_finding"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0234324</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">106160007</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A finding referable to the vagus nerve.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>vagal_nerve_finding</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_finding -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_finding">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#sign"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C1291734</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Top</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">106150003</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Deficits that arise from dysfunction of one of the twelve cranial nerves.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_reflex_finding -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_reflex_finding">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_finding"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C1287863</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">366342008</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>An abnormality due to abnormal cranial nerve reflexes.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/craniocerebral_trauma -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/craniocerebral_trauma">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/trauma"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0018674</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">82271004</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Traumatic injuries involving the cranium and intracranial structures. Injuries may be classified by whether or not the skull is penetrated (i.e., penetrating vs. nonpenetrating) or whether there is an associated hemorrhage.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cremasteric_reflex_negative -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cremasteric_reflex_negative">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cutaneous_reflex_finding"/>
        <Example_1>The cremasteric reflex was negative.</Example_1>
        <Example_2>The cremastric reflex could not be elicited.</Example_2>
        <Example_3>The cremasteric reflex was absent.</Example_3>
        <synonym_1>Stroking the thigh did not elicit the cremastric reflex.</synonym_1>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0558754</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Reflex</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">274607002</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>The cremasteric reflex is a superficial (i.e., close to the skin&apos;s surface) reflex observed in human males. This reflex is elicited by lightly stroking or poking the superior and medial (inner) part of the thigh—regardless of the direction of stroke. The normal response is an immediate contraction of the cremaster muscle that pulls up the testis ipsilaterally (on the same side of the body). The reflex utilizes sensory and motor fibers from two different nerves. When the inner thigh is stroked, sensory fibers of the ilioinguinal nerve are stimulated. These activate the motor fibers of the genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve which causes the cremaster muscle to contract and elevate the testis</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cutaneous_reflex_finding -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cutaneous_reflex_finding">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/reflex_finding"/>
        <synonym_1>cutaneous reflex</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>superficial reflex</synonym_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0278132</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Reflex</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">29922007</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Superficial reflexes are motor responses to scraping of the skin. They are graded simply as present or absent, although markedly asymmetrical responses should be considered abnormal as well. These reflexes are quite different from the muscle stretch reflexes in that the sensory signal has to not only reach the spinal cord, but also must ascend the cord to reach the brain. The motor limb then has to descend the spinal cord to reach the motor neurons. As can be seen from the description, this is a polysynaptic reflex. This can be abolished by severe lower motor neuron damage or destruction of the sensory pathways from the skin that is stimulated. However, the utility of superficial reflexes is that they are decreased or abolished by conditions that interrupt the pathways between the brain and spinal cord (such as with spinal cord damage).</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/daytime_sleepiness -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/daytime_sleepiness">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#sleep_disturbance"/>
        <Example_1>daytime sleepiness</Example_1>
        <Example_2>daytime somnulence</Example_2>
        <Example_3>excessive daytime somnulence</Example_3>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0541854</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Excessive sleepiness during the normal waking hours despite adequate rest.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>daytime somnulence</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/deafness -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/deafness">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_hearing"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0011053</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">15188001</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>lack or significant deficiency of the sense of hearing.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/decerebrate_posture -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/decerebrate_posture">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/hypertonia"/>
        <Example_1>Extensor posturing of arms and legs.</Example_1>
        <Example_2>Body rigidity with extensor posturing of limbs.</Example_2>
        <synonym_1>decerebration</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>decerebrate</synonym_2>
        <synonym_3>decerebrate posturing</synonym_3>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0231474</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">23073007</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A posture characterized body rigidity and by extension of the neck, arms, and legs. It may indicate brain stem damage and may suggest herniation of the brain.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>decerebration</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/decorticate_posture -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/decorticate_posture">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/hypertonia"/>
        <synonym_1>decorticate posturing</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>decortication</synonym_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0231475</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">85157005</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Flexion of the arms and legs in an abnormal posture.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>decortication</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/decreased_ankle_jerk_on_affected_side -->

