        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0426995</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">249885002</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>The levator veli palatini muscle emerges from the eustachian tube and the petrous temporal bone before attaching to the palatine aponeurosis, this muscle functions to elevate the soft palate during swallowing to prevent the entry of food into the nasopharynx. Impairment in this process causes a failure to elevate the soft palate.  The soft palate is moveable, consisting of muscle fibers sheathed in mucous membrane. It is responsible for closing off the nasal passages during the act of swallowing, and also for closing off the airway. During sneezing, it protects the nasal passage by diverting a portion of the excreted substance to the mouth. In humans, the uvula hangs from the end of the soft palate. Touching the uvula or the end of the soft palate evokes a strong gag reflex in most people.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_exercise_tolerance -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_exercise_tolerance">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/consitutitional_symptoms"/>
        <Example_1>reduced ability to exercise</Example_1>
        <Example_2>reduced exercise endurance</Example_2>
        <Example_3>Decreased endurance</Example_3>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0424551</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">267044007</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Reduced ability to withstand or participate in activities that induce physical or mental exertion.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_eye_movement -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_eye_movement">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#visual_disturbance"/>
        <Example_1>abnormal eye movements.</Example_1>
        <Example_2>impaired eye movements.</Example_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0028850</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">45030009</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>pathologic process affecting the voluntary or reflex-controlled movements of the eye.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_finger_to_nose_test -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_finger_to_nose_test">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/ataxia_appendicular"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0427186</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Motor</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">250063007</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Abnormality on the finger to nose test. Point-to-Point Movement Evaluation, Ask the patient to extend their index finger and touch their nose, and then touch the examiner&apos;s outstretched finger with the same finger. Ask the patient to go back and forth between touching their nose and examiner&apos;s finger. Once this is done correctly a few times at a moderate cadence, ask the patient to continue with their eyes closed. Normally this movement remains accurate when the eyes are closed. Repeat and compare to the other hand.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_gag_reflex -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_gag_reflex">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_reflex_finding"/>
        <synonym_1>impaired pharyngeal reflex</synonym_1>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0751476</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">5799008</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>The corneal reflex, also known as the blink reflex, is an involuntary blinking of the eyelids elicited by stimulation of the cornea (such as by touching or by a foreign body), though could result from any peripheral stimulus.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_glabellar_reflex -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_glabellar_reflex">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57#frontal_release_signs"/>
        <Example_1>Failure to extinguish glabellar tap reflex</Example_1>
        <Example_2>Continued blinking of the eyes when tapping the glabella</Example_2>
        <synonym_1>Myerson sign</synonym_1>
        <synonym_2>Myerson sign present.</synonym_2>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C1142017</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>Reflex</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">299802000</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>The glabellar reflex, also known as the &quot;glabellar tap sign&quot;, is a primitive reflex elicited by repetitive tapping on the forehead.[1] Subjects blink in response to the first several taps. If the blinking persists, this is known as Myerson&apos;s sign, and is abnormal and a sign of frontal release; it is often seen in people who have Parkinson&apos;s disease</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_hand_dexterity -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_hand_dexterity">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/poor_coordination"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C4313189</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>SX</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Difficulty in using hands for tasks that require dexterity and accuracy of movements.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_hearing -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_hearing">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/cranial_nerve_8_finding"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C1384666</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>CN</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">103276001</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>A general term for the complete or partial loss of the ability to hear from one or both ears.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_insight -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_insight">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/loss_of_executive_functions"/>
        <untitled-ontology-57:CUI>C0233823</untitled-ontology-57:CUI>
        <untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>MS</untitled-ontology-57:Exam_Part>
        <untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">12200008</untitled-ontology-57:SNOMED>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>Insight is the ability of patients to see themselves as they are.  When insight is impaired patients may fail to recognize their own neurological deficits such as hemiplegia, amnesia, or even blindness.</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <rdfs:seeAlso>lack_of_insight; defective_insight</rdfs:seeAlso>
    </owl:Class>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/danielhier/ontologies/2019/3/untitled-ontology-57/impaired_joint_sense -->

